      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hodgen is an unincorporated community in Le Flore County, Oklahoma, situated along the bottomlands of Conser Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hodgen%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The community was named for James M. Hodgens, a railroad official with the Kansas City, Pittsburg and Gulf Railroad.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        5,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hodgen%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The local post office originally opened as Houston in 1896 before being officially renamed Hodgen on April 25, 1910.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hodgen%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Hodgen sits on former Choctaw lands that remained under tribal governance until Oklahoma achieved statehood in 1907.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hodgen_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The historic Peter Conser House, built in 1894, symbolizes frontier life and Choctaw law enforcement in Indian Territory.",
